  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"The fair has movable category signs, adequate walking aisles, and several tables; every item still carries its own identifier and description.\", \"decision\": \"How should the display tables be organized?\", \"goal\": \"Help usable household goods reach interested neighbors while keeping contributions, claims, and final collection understandable.\", \"problem\": \"Neighbors are organizing a one-day fair for clean, usable household goods that people no longer need. Giving and taking are both optional, and no payment or matching contribution is required. Volunteers have intake space, category tables, reserve shelves, a holding area, carts, and simple item records. Contributors choose in advance whether unclaimed goods should be returned during the closing collection period or donated through an arranged pickup.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nVolunteers group household goods by function, such as storage, serving, or decoration, and label the display tables accordingly. Visitors can compare items that solve similar needs even when those goods would normally belong in different rooms.\nVolunteers group household goods by their ordinary room of use and label the display tables accordingly. Visitors looking for kitchen, bedroom, or living-room items can begin with a familiar part of the home.\nVolunteers place household goods in numbered table zones and maintain a category index showing where each type can be found. The physical arrangement can adapt to available space while visitors use the index to locate relevant items."
    }
  ],
